@page "/learn/virtualize-scroll-to-item"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Open a list at any item · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Open a list at any item</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">A link points to message #300 of a long thread. How do you open the list right there?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>Virtualize</code> can open at a chosen item, with
        <code>InitialItemIndex</code>, and scroll to any item from code, with <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code>. In .NET 10
        it always opened at the top, and the only way to move it was to scroll its box to a pixel offset.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a support thread of 420 messages, in a <code>Virtualize</code>. Some messages are one-line log
        entries. Others are long emails. A link has asked for message #300.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is the .NET 10 way: work out a pixel offset from a guessed height, and scroll to it with a line of
        JavaScript. The message you asked for is yellow, with a tag at its end. The dark readout under the list says
        where it is.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The thread, scrolled to an offset" Url="demo/scroll-to-item/incident/net10?message=300"
                Label=".NET 10: scroll to an offset" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            It has already missed: the readout says #300's header is above the top of the box, out of sight. To watch
            it happen, press <b>⟳</b> to reload: the list sits at message #1 until the circuit starts, then jumps.
            Then type <code>35</code> into <b>Jump to message #</b> and press <b>Go</b>: the box lands several messages
            late, with #35 above it.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>An offset is only as good as its guess</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> only renders the messages near the scroll position. It holds the space for the rest
        with spacers, sized from <code>ItemSize</code>: your guess at each item's height. So the page sets
        <code>ItemSize="120"</code> and scrolls to <i>(number − 1) × 120</i>. Real messages aren't 120px:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where "Jump to #35" scrolls, and where #35 is</span>
        <pre class="snip">scrollTop = (35 − 1) × 120  = <span class="err">4,080px</span>   <span class="dim">every message 120px, the guess</span>
#35 really starts near      = <span class="ok">3,480px</span>   <span class="dim">the first 34 average about 100px</span>
                              <span class="err">600px too far: the box opens in the #40s</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Near #300 it's worse. <code>Virtualize</code> places the messages it hasn't rendered by its own estimate,
        which doesn't match the guess either. And you can't fix it up afterwards: the message you want isn't on the
        page until <code>Virtualize</code> catches up with the scroll, so there's nothing to scroll into view.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Ask for the item, not a pixel</h2>
    <p>Give <code>Virtualize</code> the index to open at, and call <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> to go to one later.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">@@if (messages.Count &gt; 0)   <span class="dim">// InitialItemIndex applies once, so wait for the items</span>
{
    &lt;Virtualize @@ref="list" Items="messages" <span class="hl">InitialItemIndex="(Message ?? 1) - 1"</span>&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
}

<span class="dim">// Later, from a button, once the circuit has started (number is 1-based, like #35)</span>
<span class="hl">await list!.ScrollToItemAsync(number - 1);</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>Message</code> is the <code>?message=300</code> from the link. Both take a zero-based index, so
        message #300 is index 299. <code>Virtualize</code> renders the messages
        around that item, then lines the element itself up with the top of the box. It measures the real item, so it
        doesn't matter how tall the messages are. Here's the same thread, with the same link.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same thread, with InitialItemIndex" Url="demo/scroll-to-item/incident?message=300"
                Label=".NET 11: InitialItemIndex">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>⟳</b>. The first HTML already holds the messages around #300, and #300 is at the top, yellow.
            Type <code>35</code> into <b>Jump to message #</b> and press <b>Go</b>: #35 is at the top. Check
            <b>On the page</b> in the readout: about 30 of the 420 messages, so it's still virtualized.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        Here are both lists, in the same order as the demos. The highlighting differs: in Fig. 3 it marks the old
        offset workaround, and in Fig. 4 it marks the new API that replaces it.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ScrollToItem/LinkedThreadNet10.razor" Feature="virtualize-scroll-to-item">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's list, the .NET 10 way. Highlighted: the old workaround. There's no <code>InitialItemIndex</code>,
            so it opens at the top. Once it has rendered, it scrolls by the guessed offset, and every jump uses the
            same maths. <code>scrollToOffset</code> is the page's own JavaScript: it waits a frame until the spacers
            have a height, then sets <code>box.scrollTop = top</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ScrollToItem/LinkedThread.razor" Feature="virtualize-scroll-to-item">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's list, the .NET 11 way. Highlighted: the new API. The <code>@@if</code> on line 14 waits for the
            messages, because <code>InitialItemIndex</code> applies once. The buttons on lines 8 and 9 stay disabled
            until the circuit starts, because <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> throws before then.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Guess the offset, or stop virtualizing</h2>
    <p>
        .NET 10's <code>Virtualize</code> had no way to start anywhere but the top, and no way to scroll to an item.
        We tried each workaround in a .NET 10 app with a thread like this one:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to open a .NET 10 list at a linked message, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">scrollTop = (n - 1) * 120</pre>
                <span class="why">scroll the box to a calculated offset</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ lands a few messages late, as in Fig. 1: right only if every message is 120px</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">el.scrollIntoView()</pre>
                <span class="why">find the message by id and scroll it into view</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ finds nothing: only the first dozen messages are on the page</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@foreach (var m in messages)</pre>
                <span class="why">drop <code>Virtualize</code>, then scroll into view</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ lands exactly, with every message in the thread on the page</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The list was also blank before the circuit started: .NET 10's prerendered HTML held no messages at all, only
        a spacer. So the reader saw nothing, then the top of the thread, then a jump to roughly the right place.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The list finds the item itself</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>InitialItemIndex</code> opens the list at an item, even in the prerendered HTML.
        <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> goes to one later, rendered or not. Both land exactly, whatever the heights,
        and the list stays virtualized. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b><code>InitialItemIndex</code> applies once.</b> Changing it later does nothing. To open another thread at its own place, give each thread's <code>Virtualize</code> a <code>@@key</code>.</li>
        <li><b><code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> throws before the circuit starts.</b> It throws <code>InvalidOperationException</code> until the first interactive render. Use <code>InitialItemIndex</code> for where the list opens.</li>
        <li><b>The last call wins.</b> A new <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> cancels one still in flight, and the user scrolling beats both. Pass a <code>CancellationToken</code> to cancel one yourself.</li>
        <li><b>QuickGrid has the same pair,</b> <code>InitialItemIndex</code> and <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code>, for grids with <code>Virtualize="true"</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Early previews used other names.</b> Code written against Preview 6 says <code>InitialIndex</code> and <code>ScrollToIndexAsync</code>; rename them.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
